WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.825
Property lists, commonly called plists, are a common macOS format for configuration and state.

00:00:06.825 --> 00:00:14.971
They may store system settings, application preferences, recent items, launch configuration, saved state references, and user-specific behavior.

00:00:14.971 --> 00:00:27.079
Plists can be XML or binary, and analysts should use tools that preserve and interpret the data correctly rather than relying on casual text viewing alone.

00:00:27.079 --> 00:00:31.703
User preferences and application data often live under ~/Library.

00:00:31.703 --> 00:00:40.289
Browser profiles, productivity apps, chat tools, developer utilities, sync clients, and enterprise applications may all leave evidence there.

00:00:40.289 --> 00:00:46.674
Application support folders can contain databases, caches, configuration, logs, extensions, and local state.

00:00:46.674 --> 00:00:57.461
These artifacts can help explain what a user or application did, but they should be handled carefully because they may contain sensitive data.

00:00:57.461 --> 00:01:03.846
Recent files, saved state, and application traces can be useful for reconstructing activity.

00:01:03.846 --> 00:01:11.111
For example, an application may remember recently opened documents, windows, projects, downloads, or account associations.

00:01:11.111 --> 00:01:18.817
Browser and productivity app artifacts may show history, downloads, session data, local databases, and synchronization clues.

00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:28.724
The exact evidence depends on the application version, privacy settings, and whether data is stored locally or in a cloud service.

00:01:28.724 --> 00:01:32.907
Plists and application artifacts need context and corroboration.

00:01:32.907 --> 00:01:36.209
A preference value may be stale.

00:01:36.209 --> 00:01:42.594
A recent item may come from sync behavior rather than direct local interaction.

00:01:42.594 --> 00:01:47.217
A cached file may not prove it was viewed.

00:01:47.217 --> 00:01:56.684
Strong analysis compares plists with logs, file timestamps, quarantine attributes, user activity artifacts, and case context before making a finding.
